WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.380
I have a story for you today. That's a really
unfortunate story and it's been a difficult

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.720
thing for us, but I hope that if nothing else,
it'll be an interesting kind of peek behind

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.660
the curtain of some of the things going on
with podcasting and your data and you being

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.270
tracked when you listen to shows like the
David Pakman show. So let's just get right

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.630
into it. The David Pakman show exists on a
bunch of different platforms. Many of you

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.199
know this. We do a video show. We do an audio
show every day. The video show gets uploaded

00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:31.530
to YouTube and to Facebook. We send it to
our television affiliates. Free speech TV

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:36.510
runs it on DirecTV and dish network. That's
the video show. We also do an audio version

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:42.600
and that's distributed to a bunch of different
radio stations. We also have it on Spotify.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.930
It's an audio podcast. We've had the audio
podcast for many, many years. We work with

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:54.920
a hosting company called Libsyn for our podcast
files. So when you listen to our podcast,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:02.680
whether you're on iTunes or on Spotify or@davidpakman.com
or whatever, the files themselves exist, or

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:09.479
they live on servers that this company called
Libsyn runs. That's where the files are Lipson's

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:15.340
a great company and they're one of the reasons
that the podcast has been able to scale the

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.599
way that it has. One of the reasons we worked
with Libsyn from the beginning was that they

00:01:19.599 --> 00:01:23.779
only charge you for storage space. They don't
start a charge you for bandwidth, meaning

00:01:23.779 --> 00:01:28.890
that we pay the same amount no matter how
many downloads we get. So when you consider

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:34.200
that the podcast today is more than 10 times
bigger than it was when we started with Libsyn,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.649
uh, this is huge for us because we pay the
same amount now for our hosting as we did

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:44.389
10 years ago when the show was only a 10%
the size that it is today.

00:01:44.389 --> 00:01:50.279
Now, as many of you also might know, one of
the ways that the David Pakman show makes

00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:54.099
money is by running advertisements. So sometimes
at the end of a YouTube video there will be

00:01:54.099 --> 00:02:00.829
an ad. Sometimes if you're listening to the
podcast, there will be an ad between segments.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:05.859
These ads come from a bunch of different places,
but one of the biggest relationships that

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:12.540
we have for getting these ads on the show
is with an ad agency called Midroll. We started

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.590
working with Midroll years ago and over time,
Midroll has been acquired a couple of times

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:24.060
by other companies. They're now part of the
same company that owns the podcasting app,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.989
Stitcher, and we've done a lot of advertising
deals with them. Thankfully they've brought

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:33.140
us a significant amount of revenue. A lot
of those ads that you see at the end of a

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.760
YouTube video or that you hear on the podcast
were brought to us by mid-roll.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.689
This is a great thing. It's been a great relationship,
but a couple of months ago we were contacted

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:51.900
by mid-roll and they said, listen, we want
to transition you over to hosting your podcast

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:58.580
with a company called Omni O, M N, Y. Now,
before I even looked into this company, I

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:05.240
thought it's a little weird for an ad agency
to come in and say, Hey, you need to change

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:10.749
your podcast host if you want to continue
working with us in the way that you've been

00:03:10.749 --> 00:03:15.270
working with us. And I said to them, well,
hold on a second. I'm very happy with our

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:21.511
current podcast hosts, Libsyn. I'm not inclined
to change, but is this an ultimatum? Like

00:03:21.511 --> 00:03:27.480
are you saying either I hire a different podcast
host or you will cut off the money that we

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:33.220
get from advertisements that you send us?
Are you telling me that unless I change hosts,

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.939
I will lose all ad revenue that we get from
you?

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:40.879
And we went back and forth for a little bit
and to make a long story short, yeah, that's

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:47.250
the case. Unless we switch to this other company,
Omni to host our podcast, we will lose all

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.891
of the revenue that we get by running the
ads that Midroll has been bringing us. Now

00:03:51.891 --> 00:03:55.299
before I even go any further because we're
not even talking about your data yet, we're

00:03:55.299 --> 00:03:59.780
going to get to that in a second. Before I
go any further, this is already a very strong

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:06.139
arm action to come in and say, unless you
become the customer of this particular company,

00:04:06.139 --> 00:04:09.390
we're going to take away a whole bunch of
money, particularly when it's money that's

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:14.810
now part of our budget. We use the ad revenue
to pay salaries and to buy equipment and for

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.420
connectivity and all of the, you know, rent
studio, rent, all of that stuff.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:24.199
We abide by all of the guidelines that Midroll
puts in place. We do all the right stuff,

00:04:24.199 --> 00:04:27.200
and now they're coming in and saying, Hey,
listen, you've got to go and become a customer

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.199
of someone else if you want to keep getting
any revenue for it from us. But that's only

00:04:31.199 --> 00:04:36.800
part one. It actually gets much worse. So
I started looking into the sales pitch that

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:43.310
we were being given about Omni and I saw that
one of the big selling points was that Omni

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:50.280
would create or provide much better analytics
about you, about our audience, which could

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:56.139
then be used to sell ads and to target ads
in a better way. Now, this raised a red flag

00:04:56.139 --> 00:05:01.620
for me and as I researched it more, I learned
that the way Omni does this is via what's

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.930
called a tracking pixel. Now, without getting
into the technology, tracking pixels are used

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:09.900
in a lot of different advertising contexts.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.600
It allows for your behavior to be tracked
across the internet, often across devices

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.379
and to be used as part of the sales pitch
to potential sponsors by mid-roll. Now I asked,

00:05:21.379 --> 00:05:28.500
is that opt in for our audience? Is our audience
consenting to be tracked in that way? And

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:35.569
I was told, no, it is not you. If I made the
switch, would not have any say in being tracked

00:05:35.569 --> 00:05:41.810
in this way by Omni. Now, this is not illegal
in the United States. It's questionable, but

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:47.949
interestingly, as far as I can tell, this
is illegal in Europe on the basis of the new

00:05:47.949 --> 00:05:54.710
GDPR guidelines that now exist. So listen,
financially, I'm making the wrong decision

00:05:54.710 --> 00:06:01.780
here, but I am not going to bow to this bullying
pressure nor am I going to subject you to

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.599
being tracked, which while not illegal, I
find immoral to just switch.

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:12.729
There's no opt in and you're just being tracked
in a different way by my podcast. I get it.

00:06:12.729 --> 00:06:16.090
We know that this is happening on Facebook,
we know it's happening elsewhere, but I don't

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:21.440
want that happening with my podcast. So the
good news for you in a sense is that starting

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.210
in January you may be hearing fewer ads on
the podcast. Uh, you will likely be seeing

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:31.050
fewer ads at the end of YouTube videos because
we are going to be ending our relationship

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:35.310
with mid-roll or maybe better said they are
ending it with us because we're refusing to

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:40.500
go ahead and hire the company that they want
us to hire and to subject you to this tracking.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:45.260
But the good news is that we are going to
be sticking with Libsyn for our podcast hosting.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:50.360
We absolutely love them. I'm confident that
over the long run, this is the right decision.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.770
Even if in the short run it is financially
problematic. If in the immediate you want

00:06:54.770 --> 00:07:01.129
to help just grab a membership@joinpacman.com
as always, direct support makes us sort of

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:07.650
immune to these types of strong arm tactics
and uh, that's always the best thing to do.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:13.080
By the way, lots of the shows you likely listen
if this is the only political show you listen

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:18.770
to. Okay? If you'll listen to other lefty
political shows almost for sure, there are

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:24.199
other shows that are dealing with this exact
same pressure from Midroll just as a heads

00:07:24.199 --> 00:07:29.419
up and we'll see if any of them discuss it.
Every show will make the decision that's best

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:34.250
for them. And I think that even though it
will be difficult for us, this is the right

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.599
decision. Let me know what you think.

